verdict=IN-SCOPE: items C and D of the approved increment release, with the parallel module, the spots exclusion and the ratchet all absent, and both buffer-adoption correctness risks checked.
# Independent scope judge, reading the plan by path and the staged diff.
# 
# Two files only: src/export.ts and the module map's regenerated line count.
# 
# C is implemented on both sides. The 16-bit path adopts the stitched result
# instead of setting it into a buffer made in advance, and both paths now allocate
# only on the fallback, which is the hoist the plan names.
# 
# D is implemented as the plan states it: the drawing context is no longer
# asserted non-null, a refusal throws a named error carrying the size, and the
# surface is released on every exit path including a failed encode. The
# area clamp against a probe-measured ceiling is correctly deferred, because that
# number does not exist until the probe has run on a device.
# 
# The watermark layer's surface is released at both sites. Judged in scope: same
# defect class, same helper, same path, no new capability.
# 
# The band result types are tightened to real buffers. Judged a consequence of C
# rather than unrelated: the parallel module already declared that return type and
# already carried a cast to reach it, and the direct assignment now type-checks as
# what the wire actually guarantees.
# 
# Absent: the parallel module, the spots exclusion, any ratchet, main.ts and every
# walk.
# 
# Correctness, checked by reading the whole surrounding branch rather than the
# diff alone. No path reaches a use of either buffer while it is null: every read
# sits after the line that allocates on fallback, with no branch or early return
# between. No wrong-length adoption: this is the top-level call, so the band is
# the whole frame and the stitched result is sized identically to what the eager
# allocation would have produced.
